-- ADVANCED / MEDIUM — Stimulus: Bioluminescence
(gen_random_uuid(),'MULTIPLE_CHOICE',7,'READING','Comprehension – Literal',3,
'Bioluminescence is the remarkable ability of certain living organisms to produce and emit light. This natural phenomenon occurs in a diverse range of creatures, including fireflies, deep-sea fish, jellyfish and some species of fungi. The light is produced through a chemical reaction involving a compound called luciferin and an enzyme called luciferase. For many organisms, bioluminescence serves important purposes: predators use it to lure prey into the depths of the ocean, while some species use light signals to attract mates. In shallow waters, bioluminescent plankton create breathtaking displays, turning breaking waves a ghostly blue. Scientists are increasingly studying bioluminescence for practical applications, including medical imaging and the development of biosensors.','TEXT',
'ADVANCED','MEDIUM',
'What is bioluminescence?','{"A":"A type of light produced by electrical devices","B":"The ability of living organisms to produce and emit light","C":"Light reflected by organisms underwater","D":"The study of light in marine environments"}','{"value":"B"}','The passage defines bioluminescence as the ability of living organisms to produce and emit light.','PUBLISHED',NOW(),NULL),
